The project began with identifying a common problem in educational institutions: manual attendance marking. Taking attendance by roll call or paper sheets consumes five to ten minutes of every class, allows students to mark proxy attendance for absent friends, and makes monthly report generation slow and error-prone. Several solutions were considered during brainstorming, including QR-code attendance, RFID cards, and face recognition. QR codes were easy and inexpensive but could be shared with absent students, while RFID cards required extra hardware and could be lost or swapped. Face recognition was finally selected because it is contactless, accurate, and very difficult to fake. A SWOT analysis showed that the system's strengths were automation and real-time reporting, its main weakness was dependence on camera quality, its opportunities included expansion to offices and events, and its main threat was privacy concern over biometric data.
